--- body ---
## The ruling, 2026-09-09

Superseding Action 4 below (the Zotero collection offer) and the API option
recorded in the reimagine addenda. Reached in conversation, not from a
written brief; recorded here verbatim in spirit rather than left implicit in
the log.

**Structure.** A new collapsible section, "Unindexed attachments," under the
existing Details layer. Under it, one collapsible subsection per class.
Classes are grouped by REPAIR — the action, if any, that resolves them — not
by internal status or mechanism. This is a sharper cut than "problem class"
in the ticket as filed: it is what lets two different underlying causes
share one subsection when their fix is the same, and it is what keeps a
document that hits more than one obstacle simple to reason about — classify
by whichever obstacle currently blocks it, and let a fix reveal the next.

**Per-subsection controls.** Two buttons, both inert until operated, neither
writing anything: "Show in Zotero" (a library-pane selection, ephemeral,
per library — the mechanism Fable's reimagine note argued for in place of a
collection) and "Export list to clipboard" (reusing the existing
`copySDTText`/`journal-copy` plumbing, one line per item). No Zotero
collection is created by this feature; if the author wants one, "Show in
Zotero" already hands him a selection to build it from natively.

**No API.** Ruled out directly ("No API"). The enumerate-by-class capability
argued for in the reimagine note's second addendum is not built as part of
this ticket; conception/0744-reimagining-problem-class-grouping.md and
ticket 0758's cross-reference stand as the record of that option, not as
work this ticket owns.

**Classes confirmed so far**, remedy text as given, repair-grouped:

| Class | Remedy (as given) | Source status(es) |
|---|---|---|
| No attachment | "must fetch from web" | new — item has zero attached files, not an attachment-level status at all |
| Need sync | "hit the button and wait" | `missing-source`, `attachmentLinkMode` imported/embedded — verified, see Derisking findings |
| Missing file / broken link | "sync from the other machine" | `missing-source`, `attachmentLinkMode` linked — verified, see Derisking findings |
| Mismatched type | re-save with the true type, or OCR | `unsupported`, sniffed bytes contradict declared type (ticket 0740) |
| No extractor for this format | nothing to be done inside Zotero | `unsupported`, genuinely no processor (e.g. `.docx`) |
| No text layer | OCR | `current` with near-zero extracted content — confirmed by measurement, see Derisking findings; needs ticket 0760's new status to exist before this subsection has anything to read |
| Extraction failed this session | tried again automatically the next time Zotero starts (ticket 0740's own ruling) | `failed-session`, own subsection — added per Opus review, see below |
| Stored index from an older version | regenerated automatically once a feature next needs it (e.g. opening the document) | `unsupported-pack`, `packVersion`/`schemaVersion` behind the running Zotero — split by direction, see below |
| Stored index from a newer version | nothing to be done here; the machine that wrote it is ahead of this one | `unsupported-pack`, the other direction — split by direction, see below |
| Could not be examined | one line per item, composed from the error's CLASS (`classifyError`), not the raw platform message | `inspection-error`, own subsection — corrected per Opus review, see below |

"Each error to its own section" (author): confirmed, every status above is
its own subsection, never folded into another — including `failed-session`,
missing from the table until the Opus review below caught it, and
`unsupported-pack`, now split rather than folded, since its two directions
carry opposite certainty.

## Derisking findings, 2026-09-09 (resolves the three Open items above)

All three were checked against real Zotero source and real library data
(`~/data/Zotero-fresh/zotero.sqlite`, Zotero not running, read-only) rather
than guessed. None needed the author's judgment — they were facts.

1. **Need-sync / Missing-file discriminator: `attachmentLinkMode` confirmed,
   `attachmentSyncState` confirmed too weak to use.** Every query driving
   Zotero's own file-sync machinery, read directly out of
   `chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/storage/storageLocal.js` inside `app/omni.ja`,
   is scoped to `linkMode IN (LINK_MODE_IMPORTED_FILE, LINK_MODE_IMPORTED_URL,
   LINK_MODE_EMBEDDED_IMAGE)` — linked attachments never appear in any of
   them. `attachmentLinkMode` is sound, not a guess. The alternative
   considered, `item.attachmentSyncState` (a cheap, synchronous, already-
   loaded field), was measured and rejected: 200 of 15,855 imported/URL
   attachments (1.3%) read `IN_SYNC` while genuinely absent from disk — it
   only refreshes at Zotero's own sync-time scan, not continuously, so it is
   stale exactly when this feature would need it to be current. Use
   `attachmentLinkMode` alone. All 5 `LINK_MODE_LINKED_FILE` attachments in
   the sampled library are absent — consistent with "sync from the other
   machine" being the only real remedy for that branch, never automatic.
2. **No text layer: confirmed silent `current`, not `failed-session`.**
   Zotero's own `fulltextItems.totalChars`/`indexedChars` were checked first
   as a cheap proxy and found NULL for every PDF row in this library/version
   — dead here, not usable. Measured directly instead: of 1,195 existing
   `.zotero-sdt-cache` packs sampled, 61 (5.1%) carry under 20 characters of
   `pdftotext`-extracted text across their PDF's first 5 pages despite the
   pack existing and being well-formed. Filed as its own correctness ticket,
   0760 — this affects "Files indexed X/Y" today, independent of whether
   this ticket ships, and this ticket's "No text layer" subsection has
   nothing to read until 0760's new status exists.
3. **"No attachment": sized at 1,991 of 13,407 bibliographic items** (real
   spread: journalArticle 427, report 355, bookSection 288, conferencePaper
   245, …), via one `Zotero.DB.queryAsync` join against `deletedItems` and
   `itemAttachments`, near-instant against 31,325 total items. Cheap; no new
   capability beyond adapting a query pattern already used elsewhere in this
   codebase.
4. **`attachmentFilename` confirmed: free, and settles Action 2's cost
   question.** Read directly out of `app/omni.ja`'s
   `chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/data/item.js:3571-3592`: the getter derives
   from `attachmentPath`, itself part of `_primaryDataSQLParts` — joined into
   the base item query Zotero always runs, not a lazy tier requiring
   `loadData()`. `inspect()` already calls `Zotero.Items.getAsync(id)` first,
   so this field is already on the object at zero extra cost, for every one
   of the four title-less classes, including `missing-source` (it names the
   file even though the file itself is absent). Timed the equivalent full
   query shape directly against a copy of a real library (SQL approximation
   of Zotero's own JOIN, not the live JS API, but the same shape and fields):
   **25.5 ms at full-library scale (16,715 attachments)**, roughly six times
   the size of the four affected classes combined. Resolve titles on every
   sweep; the lazy-per-expanded-subsection fallback Action 2 named as a
   fallback is unnecessary. One residual case: `LINK_MODE_LINKED_URL`
   attachments (`attachmentPath` null) return an empty string, not a crash,
   but not a usable identifier either — small population, name it plainly
   ("no filename on record") rather than showing a blank row.
5. **Multi-library spread: confirmed real, not an edge case (Open item 2).**
   Measured on a 3-library fixture (1 user, 2 group): the title-less
   populations span more than one library in both directions checked — the
   `unsupported`-shaped population alone is user 4,006 · group-A 3,365 ·
   group-B 69, and `missing-source`-shaped is user 447 · group-A 174. A
   group library's population was comparable in size to the user library's,
   not a rounding error. This doesn't resolve the open design question (how
   "Show in Zotero" behaves when a class spans libraries — still the
   author's call, `ZoteroPane.selectItems` cannot cross libraries) but it
   settles that the question is live on day one, not a rare case safe to
   defer.

## Opus review findings, 2026-09-09

An independent design review (before implementation; no code existed to
review). Findings that changed the design are folded into the table and
sections above; recorded here so the reasoning survives, not just the
conclusion.

**Fixed in this revision:**

- **`inspection-error`'s raw error text is a real defect, not a nitpick —
  confirmed and fixed by redacting the filepath (author instruction).**
  bootstrap.js's own comment on the platform-throw path states a platform
  message "names the path it failed on" — `String(error)` on such a throw
  puts an absolute filesystem path into the dialog and the clipboard export,
  the latter of which this ticket's own reimagine note anticipates being
  pasted into a bug report or an agent prompt. It is also a tone risk on its
  own terms: a raw platform message reads as an alarm, which the rest of
  this feature works to avoid. Fix: `classifyError` (bootstrap.js:419-426,
  already used at the cache-error site) already exists for exactly this and
  needs no new redaction logic — checked directly rather than assumed: it
  reads only `error.name`, validated against `/^[\w.$-]{1,64}$/`, and never
  touches `error.message` at all, so a stored filepath cannot reach the
  output by construction (its own comment records three prior attempts to
  scrub prose token-by-token, all of which leaked; not carrying the message
  is what finally worked). `inspection-error`'s subsection therefore shows
  one composed sentence per error CLASS (e.g. the class name itself, or a
  short composed phrase keyed on it), never `error.message`, never a path.
- **`unsupported-pack`'s remedy is direction-dependent.** `packVersionMismatch`
  is a `!==` comparison and fires symmetrically whether the stored pack is
  older or newer than what this running Zotero declares. "Regenerated
  automatically" is true only in the older direction; a pack newer than this
  Zotero (synced from a machine on a later version) will not regenerate here
  at all. Both versions are already in hand at the comparison site, so
  splitting by sign is free. Split into two rows above.
- **`failed-session` (39 items) had no subsection.** The status→class
  mapping was not exhaustive and had no residual bucket; this status simply
  fell out of the design silently. Added as its own subsection, grounded in
  ticket 0740's own ruling (cleared each restart, retried automatically) —
  no guess needed, the remedy is already the documented behaviour.
- **Empty subsections should not render.** Adopts the prior art the
  reimagine note already found (Zotero's own Duplicate Items / Unfiled Items
  / Retracted Items views appear only when non-empty) and the ruling had not
  carried over. Eight always-present subsections, several reading "0
  documents," is the checklist/backlog framing the tone rule forbids.
- **The buttons act on the full class, never only the capped visible
  titles.** Unstated before; selecting or exporting only what's shown in a
  capped dialog list while a class holds thousands more would be a silent
  undercount. Both controls always operate on the complete class.
- **A "show all" expansion inside the dialog**, since the cap otherwise makes
  "read the whole list" require leaving the plugin for the clipboard export
  — cheap, the subsection is already a `<details>`, nesting one more costs
  nothing.

**Recorded as still open, for the author:**

- **The section's own name.** "Unindexed attachments" includes "No
  attachment," whose members have no attachment at all — the heading and one
  of its own subsections disagree about what an "attachment" is. Rename the
  section, or place "No attachment" outside it as its own statement with its
  own count. Naming is the author's call, per this ticket's own precedent
  (Action 1 below).
- **Multi-library selection.** `ZoteroPane.selectItems` cannot select across
  libraries, and a class of 2,600 can span more than one. Undecided whether
  "Show in Zotero" splits per library, is disabled when a class spans more
  than one, or something else.
- **`missing-source`'s inclusion was argued through, not silently overridden**
  — recorded here since the review is right that the ticket itself didn't
  say so: the reimagine note raised ticket 0740's "ordinary fact, not a
  failure" ruling as a reason to exclude it; asked directly, the resolution
  reached was to include it under the neutral "Unindexed attachments" framing
  (rather than "problems") with fact-only wording and no remedy imperative
  for the sync-pending case — which is what "Need sync"/"hit the button and
  wait" already is. Recorded as the reasoning, not left implicit.
- **Not acted on**: i18n/pluralization debt (every new string is an
  `SDT_TEXT` entry, subject to the existing vocabulary-ban test — stated
  explicitly in Actions below rather than assumed), and the accessibility
  notes (buttons inside a closed `<details>` are correctly out of the tab
  order by construction; the clipboard button must reuse `journal-copy`'s
  failure path, not only its success path) — both folded into Actions/Test
  below rather than needing further discussion.

## Context

The sitter's census classifies every attachment, and the Details layer now
prints that classification as an account: one row per status, in words, with
the total under the rows that make it up (ticket 0693, second pass). Several of
those rows are populated by documents the sitter will never index — 2 600
`unsupported`, 367 `missing-source`, 39 `failed-session` on the author's own
library — and the account says how many there are and nothing about what, if
anything, the user could do about them.

The author's guidance, in session on 2026-09-08 while ticket 0742's dialog work
was being scoped, is that the window should be helpful about that. It is
recorded verbatim in 0742's log; the two entries are:

> for a document the sitter classifies as unsupported or refused, the
> dialog/details layer should be helpful about what the user could do to make it
> indexable if they want to — e.g. what changes would let a mismatched-type or
> unextractable document succeed — but this must stay informational, never
> directive. No nagging, no "fix this" framing, no implied obligation; the user
> is told what is possible, not what to do.

> group the affected documents by problem class (a class being a shared reason
> and a shared fix — e.g. "scanned image, no text layer" versus "snapshot failed
> extraction"), state per class what would resolve it, and list the affected
> items by title under that class. Then offer, not impose: ask whether the user
> would like a Zotero collection created from that class's items, so they have
> something concrete to act on later if they choose to — never automatic, never
> a nag to act now.

## What a problem class is, and why it is not a status

`SDT_STATUS_CLASSES` partitions by what a status means for coverage arithmetic.
A problem class partitions by shared REASON and shared FIX, and the two do not
line up. `unsupported` alone covers at least three: an attachment format no
extractor handles at all (a `.docx`, nothing to be done inside Zotero); a file
whose declared type is contradicted by its own leading bytes, which since ticket
0740 the sniffer catches — on the author's library eleven page scans stored as
`text/html` over JPEG bytes, where the fix is re-saving the attachment with its
true type or running OCR; and a scanned PDF with no text layer, where the fix is
OCR and nothing else. One status, three remedies. Conversely `missing-source`
and one flavour of `invalid-pack` may share the same remedy.

So the class is a new mapping, and where it is computed matters: the sniffer's
verdict (`SDT_SOURCE_MAGIC`, `sniffSDTSource`) already distinguishes the second
case from the first and is discarded — the call site collapses a mismatch to
`unsupported` and keeps nothing. That verdict is the cheapest available input
and is the natural place to start.

## The tone constraint is the hard part

"Informational, never directive" is not a wording pass to be applied at the end;
it decides the shape. A per-class line that reads as a task list is wrong even
if every sentence is factually correct. Two rules that follow from the author's
words: no imperative mood in the remedy text, and no count presented as a
backlog. The collection offer is a control the user may operate, never a
prompt that appears because the sitter thinks he should.

**Restated and sharpened, 2026-09-09 (author): "Do not frame anything as
problem, only facts reporting."** Not only the remedy sentences — the word
"problem" (and its synonyms: "issue," "error" as a noun applied to a
document, "failure" applied to a document rather than to an extraction
attempt) is banned from every heading, subsection title and button label in
this feature, not only the per-class remedy text the original guidance
named. "Unindexed attachments," the section name, already reads as fact
rather than complaint; the same bar applies to whatever the section is
eventually renamed to (Open item 1) and to every subsection title.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `describeSDTCensusAccount` (the account
  this extends), `SDT_TEXT`, the Details layer built in `openDialog`'s
  `populate`, `sniffSDTSource` and `SDT_SOURCE_MAGIC` (the discarded verdict),
  `classifyError` (the composer `inspection-error`'s fix reuses), `inspect()`'s
  early-return branches (`~1911, 1922, 1926, 1932` — where titles are absent,
  see Action 2), `journal-copy`'s success/failure status-line pattern
  (`~1230-1236`, `copySDTText`)
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `SDT_STATUS_CLASSES`, and `inspect`'s
  result shape in bootstrap.js, which is where a class would have to be carried
- `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` — drives the account against a real sweep
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py` — `UI_SITES` and the vocabulary ban; a new dialog
  site must be added there or it stops being covered
- Ticket 0740 — the label-contradiction population, and why it is `unsupported`
- Ticket 0742 — the Details layer this hangs in, and the author's guidance in
  its log
- Ticket 0760 — the "No text layer" status this ticket's own subsection needs

## Actions

1. Settle the two remaining Open items with the author: the section's own
   name ("Unindexed attachments" contradicting its own "No attachment" row),
   and multi-library selection behaviour for "Show in Zotero" — confirmed
   live on day one, not a rare case, see Derisking finding 5.
2. **Resolve titles for the four classes that currently have none — decided
   and settled.** `unsupported`, both `missing-source` flavours, and
   `inspection-error` all return from `inspect()` before title resolution
   runs (Opus review's blocker finding). Use `item.attachmentFilename`
   (Derisking finding 4): already-loaded primary data, zero extra cost, and
   correctly names even a `missing-source` attachment whose file is absent.
   Resolve on every sweep — measured at 25.5 ms at full-library scale, no
   lazy fallback needed. One residual case to handle explicitly: a
   `LINK_MODE_LINKED_URL` attachment's `attachmentFilename` is an empty
   string; render "no filename on record" rather than a blank row for that
   population. Carry the class (with the sniffer's verdict kept, not
   collapsed) out of `inspect()` at the same time.
3. Add the item-level "No attachment" scan (sized: 1,991 items, see Derisking
   findings), separate from the attachment-walking census.
4. Depends on ticket 0760: the "No text layer" subsection reads whatever new
   status 0760 introduces; this ticket does not duplicate that measurement or
   that fix.
5. Render "Unindexed attachments" in the Details layer: one collapsible
   subsection per class from the ruling's table, each with its remedy in the
   indicative — for `inspection-error`, one composed sentence per error CLASS
   via `classifyError`, never the raw platform message — the affected titles
   under it (with a "show all" expansion past the cap), a "Show in Zotero"
   button and an "Export list to clipboard" button, both always acting on the
   full class regardless of what's visible under the cap. A subsection with
   zero members does not render, and the whole section does not render if
   every class is empty. Every new string is an `SDT_TEXT` entry, covered by
   the existing vocabulary-ban test (`tests/test_sdt_sitter.py`'s `UI_SITES`)
   — add the new dialog site there in the same change.
6. **Tooltip on each button (author).** Both "Show in Zotero" and "Export
   list to clipboard" get a `title` attribute clarifying their behaviour —
   what they act on (the full class, not only what's visible under the cap;
   which library, once Open item 1's multi-library question is settled) and
   that neither writes anything. Read-only clarification text, not a second
   place the tone constraint could be violated — no "problem" language here
   either.
7. No Zotero collection offer, no API — both ruled out. Do not build either
   as part of this ticket.

## Test

- Red first: a fixture library holding one document of each class renders one
  subsection per class under "Unindexed attachments," each naming its remedy
  (or, for `inspection-error`, its composed per-class sentence) and listing
  its titles — including the four classes that have no title today, proving
  Action 2 actually supplies one via `attachmentFilename`.
- Red first: a `LINK_MODE_LINKED_URL` fixture (empty `attachmentFilename`)
  renders "no filename on record," not a blank row.
- Red first: a document whose bytes contradict its declared type lands in
  "Mismatched type" apart from one whose format has no extractor at all —
  same status today, different class, and a test that only exercises one of
  them cannot see the collapse come back.
- Red first: an empty class renders no subsection; a library where every
  class is empty renders no "Unindexed attachments" section at all.
- Red first: "Show in Zotero" selects without writing, and "Export list to
  clipboard" writes to the clipboard and nowhere else — both inert until
  operated, meaning specifically that expanding a subsection's `<details>`
  alone triggers neither.
- Red first: both buttons act on the FULL class on a fixture whose class
  exceeds the in-dialog cap, not only the titles rendered before it.
- Red first: `attachmentLinkMode` actually separates a stored-and-missing
  attachment from a linked-and-missing one on a real or faithfully mocked
  host — the derisking findings above are evidence for the design, not a
  substitute for a committed test.
- Red first: `inspection-error`'s rendered text never contains a filesystem
  path or a raw platform message, on a fixture that reproduces the kind of
  throw bootstrap.js's own comment names (a permission error naming a path).
- Red first: both buttons carry a non-empty `title` attribute.
- A mechanical sweep for "problem"/"issue"/"error" (as a noun about a
  document)/"failure" (about a document) across every new `SDT_TEXT` entry
  this feature adds catches the word-level violation; it cannot catch a
  sentence that implies blame without using a banned word, which is why tone
  stays a review item too.
- Tone is not testable by assertion beyond the word sweep above. It is a
  review item, and the author is the reviewer.

## Verification

- [ ] The class list matches the ruling's table as corrected by the Opus
      review (10 rows, `failed-session` and the split `unsupported-pack`
      included), recorded before the rendering is built
- [ ] Every one of the four title-less classes (Action 2) lists real titles,
      not blanks
- [ ] Each subsection states a remedy without an imperative and without a
      backlog framing, except `inspection-error`, which shows a composed
      per-error-class sentence — never a raw platform message or a path
- [ ] "Show in Zotero" and "Export list to clipboard" are both inert until
      operated, act on the full class, and neither writes anything, ever
- [ ] Both buttons carry a clarifying tooltip
- [ ] Empty classes and an entirely-empty section render nothing
- [ ] No section heading, subsection title or button label uses "problem,"
      "issue," or "error"/"failure" applied to a document
- [ ] No Zotero collection is created anywhere in this feature
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- The plugin still never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  settings, never replaces a source attachment, and — per the ruling — never
  writes to the library at all. Neither of this feature's two controls is a
  write; there is no exception left to scope.
- The census account's total still equals the rows above it: grouping is a
  second reading of the same classification, never a second arithmetic. The
  item-level "No attachment" count is not part of that arithmetic and is
  never implied to be — its members are outside the census by construction.
- No nagging surface. Nothing here may appear unasked outside the Details layer.
- No filesystem path, and no raw platform error message, reaches the dialog
  or the clipboard export.

## Exit criteria

The Details layer shows a section (name resolved per Open item 1) with one
collapsible subsection per class from the ruling's table as corrected by the
Opus review — ten rows, none empty-rendered, none using "problem" or a
synonym anywhere in its heading or controls — each stating its remedy in the
indicative, or for `inspection-error` a composed per-error-class sentence via
`classifyError` with no path and no raw platform text, with real titles
listed underneath (including the four classes that have none today) and a
"show all" past the in-dialog cap. Each subsection carries a "Show in
Zotero" and an "Export list to clipboard" button, both inert until operated,
both carrying a clarifying tooltip, both acting on the full class regardless
of the cap, and neither writing anything. No Zotero collection, no API. The
two remaining Open items (section name, multi-library selection) and the
title-resolution cost measurement are resolved — measured, not guessed —
before this is considered done, and the author has read the rendered tone.
